{"body":{"actor":"w1","audit_root":"907f58bb9c5a6986a30ed308feea93edaea6243e1b1fb88f2d5b54e1d70b09ca","command":{"allowed_records":["055c3b232f4d91ca3898c658c421637b3ec6181635f6eec74ea53ca4449c86b5","1b576cda6c83b2d686e99f94658dd54ba9ac4cd1e9588de39c749a5f62ecf65a","252ccbde209a7ee22c2b1ddc484722fa36c83c7a544d477de23d06947e632715","9dc1aaecb6168841b3eac369a9e7acaccc09c5fe8f6fad798fcb04ab8fb17c17","bf785f29bacecc0fe23fee3cbc0160bab2e8faec36d246c51403c80716cea991","e373dbcafa13f1db78dd7ffcf2f76df7e2f6c4fd27201ed91a89579d0d659112","f495a5d0166ab0609fae7ba60165a23a26a0246a98c874b8d15b7abff3812968"],"directive":"TQ129-146d80824f3be926","kind":"submit","lines":[{"doc_id":"d_34f79fc515679e3aad5c296e9d9d5778e565fdf5e53b905d53c0850fb198fe54","exact_quote":"outlines why token audience validation is crucial and why token passthrough is explicitly forbidden.","kind":"observe","local_id":"t129_o1","terms":["token passthrough prohibition","security best practices cross-reference"]},{"doc_id":"d_34f79fc515679e3aad5c296e9d9d5778e565fdf5e53b905d53c0850fb198fe54","exact_quote":"The MCP server **MUST NOT** pass through the token it received from the MCP client.","kind":"observe","local_id":"t129_o2","terms":["token passthrough prohibition","upstream API tokens"]},{"doc_id":"d_34f79fc515679e3aad5c296e9d9d5778e565fdf5e53b905d53c0850fb198fe54","exact_quote":"If the MCP server not only accepts tokens with incorrect audiences but also forwards these unmodified tokens to downstream services","kind":"observe","local_id":"t129_o3","terms":["token passthrough definition","downstream forwarding"]},{"doc_id":"d_34f79fc515679e3aad5c296e9d9d5778e565fdf5e53b905d53c0850fb198fe54","exact_quote":"The access token used at the upstream API is a separate token, issued by the upstream authorization server.","kind":"observe","local_id":"t129_o4","terms":["separate upstream token","OAuth client role"]},{"as_of":null,"based_on":["t129_o2","t129_o4"],"kind":"claim","local_id":"t129_c1","object":"token received from the MCP client","predicate":"MUST NOT pass through","stance":"confirmed","subject":"MCP server acting as OAuth client to upstream APIs","text":"The excerpt does define the forbidden behavior: an MCP server MUST NOT pass through the token it received from the MCP client; upstream API tokens must be separate tokens issued by the upstream authorization server."},{"as_of":null,"based_on":["t129_o3"],"kind":"claim","local_id":"t129_c2","object":"accepting wrong-audience tokens and forwarding them unmodified downstream","predicate":"is defined as","stance":"confirmed","subject":"token passthrough","text":"Token passthrough is characterized as accepting tokens with incorrect audiences and forwarding them unmodified to downstream services, risking the confused deputy problem."},{"as_of":null,"based_on":["t129_o1"],"kind":"claim","local_id":"t129_c3","object":"why token audience validation is crucial and token passthrough explicitly forbidden","predicate":"outlines","stance":"reported","subject":"Security Best Practices document","text":"The excerpt delegates rationale to the Security Best Practices document, stating it outlines why token audience validation is crucial and why token passthrough is explicitly forbidden."},{"about":["token passthrough","Security Best Practices document"],"kind":"question","local_id":"t129_q1","needed":"Fetch /specification/2025-11-25/basic/security_best_practices#token-passthrough and quote its token-passthrough section; not present in these sections.","text":"Does the linked Security Best Practices document's token-passthrough section add prohibitions beyond the excerpt's MUST NOT pass through client token, and does it define passthrough for non-OAuth or alternative transports?"}],"scope":{"docs":["d_34f79fc515679e3aad5c296e9d9d5778e565fdf5e53b905d53c0850fb198fe54"],"ranges":[["d_34f79fc515679e3aad5c296e9d9d5778e565fdf5e53b905d53c0850fb198fe54",30038,35989],["d_34f79fc515679e3aad5c296e9d9d5778e565fdf5e53b905d53c0850fb198fe54",35989,413